    <solution><alert>(a)</alert>
<m>\int_{0}^{6}\,e^x\,dx = e^x\bigg|_{0}^{6} = e^6 - e^0 
  \approx 402.4288\ldots</m>




<alert>(b)</alert>
Computing the sums directly, since <m>\Delta x=3</m>, we have



LEFT(2) <m>= 3\cdot e^0 + 3\cdot e^{3} \approx 63.2567</m> 


RIGHT(2) <m>= 3\cdot e^{3} + 3\cdot e^{6} \approx 1270.54</m> 


TRAP(2) <m>= \frac 1 2 ( 63.2567 + 1270.54 ) \approx 666.9</m> 


MID(2) <m>= 3\cdot e^{1.5} + 3\cdot e^{3\cdot 1.5} \approx 283.497</m> 


and
SIMP(4) = (2 MID(2) + TRAP(2))/3 <m>\approx 411.297</m>.



And the errors are just the actual value minus the approximate values,
or



error(LEFT(2)) = 339.172; error(RIGHT(2)) = -868.111;
error(TRAP(2)) = -264.471; error(MID(2)) = 118.933;
and error(SIMP(4)) = -8.868.




<alert>(c)</alert>
Similarly, since <m>\Delta x = 0.75</m>, we have



LEFT(4) = 173.377; error = 229.052 


RIGHT(4) = 777.02; error = -374.591 


TRAP(4) = 475.198; error = -72.769 


MID(4) = 367.038; error = 35.391 


SIMP(8) = 403.091; error = -0.662.




<alert>(d)</alert>
For LEFT and RIGHT, we expect the error to go down by 1/2, and this
is very roughly what we see.  For MID and TRAP, we expect the error to
go down by 1/4, and this is approximately what we see.  For SIMP, we
expect the error to go down by 1/16, again as observed.



</solution>
